course: everyday
lesson: 79
title: Germs, sneezes and dressing for the weather
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Colds and flu are caused by viruses, tiny germs that travel in the drops we sneeze and cough out and in our spit, and then sit on hands and on the things we touch.
explain: So we sneeze into a tissue or our elbow, bin used tissues straight away, wash our hands, and keep our own cup and bottle.
explain: Many sayings about germs are myths: wet hair does not give you a cold, food on the floor picks up germs at once, and antibiotics do not cure colds, because they only work on bacteria.
explain: Dressing well protects the body too: being wet makes you cold fast, feet need shoes that fit, and the weather can change during the day.
explain: A boy in Dublin sees sunshine in the morning but the forecast says showers later, so he packs a raincoat and a jumper in his bag, and is warm and dry on the way home.
fact: Sneezes spray germs a long way, so you sneeze into a tissue or your elbow, not your hands.
fact: A used tissue goes in the bin straight away, and then you wash your hands.
fact: In English-speaking countries people often say «bless you» after a sneeze, and the person says «thank you».
fact: A cough that lasts for weeks or makes breathing hard needs a doctor, because it can be asthma or an infection.
fact: Colds, flu and cold sores spread through spit, so everyone uses their own cup or bottle.
fact: The five-second rule is a myth: food that falls on the floor picks up germs at once.
fact: Keeping a little distance from someone who is coughing and washing hands often lowers your chance of catching their illness.
fact: Colds come from viruses, not from going out with wet hair.
fact: Antibiotics work only on bacteria, not viruses, and taking them when not needed makes them work less well later; a cold gets better by itself with rest and drinks.
fact: Being wet makes you cold quickly, so a waterproof coat and boots are worn on rainy days.
fact: The weather can change during the day, so a jumper or raincoat is packed when the forecast says so.
fact: Proper shoes that fit protect and support the feet, and children's feet grow fast, so tight shoes are replaced.
fact: Clothes go on in order: underwear first, then clothes, then socks and shoes.
exercise: You are about to sneeze and have no tissue. Where should you sneeze? | Into your elbow | Into your hands | Into the air in front of you | Down towards the floor
exercise: Why not sneeze into your hands? | Your hands then spread the germs onto things | Hands make the sneeze much louder for everyone | It hurts your fingers | Sneezes have no germs in them
exercise: What should you do with a used tissue? | Put it in the bin straight away and wash your hands | Keep it in your pocket for later, in case you need it again | Leave it on the desk | Give it to a friend to throw away
exercise: Someone near you sneezes. What is a polite thing to say? | Bless you | Go away | Be quiet | You are ill
exercise: Your cough has gone on for weeks and you get out of breath. What should you do? | Tell a parent so a doctor can check | Keep it quiet so you can still play | Drink cola until it stops | Run more to make your lungs stronger
exercise: A friend offers you a sip from her water bottle. What is best? | Use your own bottle | Share it, friends' germs are harmless | Share it if she is not coughing yet | Wipe the top with your hand first
exercise: Your biscuit falls on the floor and you pick it up in two seconds. Is it clean? | No, germs get on it at once | Yes, if it is less than five seconds | Yes, if nobody saw it fall | Yes, if it is a dry biscuit
exercise: A classmate is coughing and sneezing a lot. What can you do to stay well? | Keep a little distance and wash your hands often | Share his snacks so you build up your strength against his germs | Hold your breath all day | Sit right next to him to help
exercise: Your grandma says going out with wet hair gives you a cold. What is true? | Colds come from viruses, not wet hair | Wet hair always gives you a cold | Wet hair gives you flu instead of a cold | Only long wet hair gives colds
exercise: Can antibiotics cure a cold? | No, antibiotics work on bacteria, colds are viruses | Yes, they cure any illness you can catch, including colds | Yes, but only a big dose | No, colds can only be cured by sweets
exercise: What should you wear on a rainy school day? | A waterproof coat and boots | A cotton hoodie and trainers | Shorts, because wet legs dry fast | Your thickest wool jumper alone
exercise: The forecast says it will turn cold this afternoon. What should you do? | Pack a jumper or raincoat in your bag | Wear only a T-shirt and hope | Stay home all day | Wear three coats at once in the morning
exercise: Your family is going on a long hike. Which shoes should you wear? | Proper shoes that fit well | Flip-flops, they are cool and light | Your smart shoes for parties | Slippers, they are soft
exercise: Your shoes have started to pinch your toes. What should you do? | Tell a parent you need a bigger size | Keep wearing them until they stretch | Cut holes for your toes | Wear them only on one foot
exercise: In what order should you get dressed? | Underwear, then clothes, then socks and shoes | Shoes first, then socks, then trousers and the rest | Coat first, then everything under it | Any order, it makes no difference
test: A boy in Liverpool sneezes into his hand and then hands out pencils. What could happen? | His germs could spread to the whole table | The pencils would kill the germs | Nothing, germs need a full minute to get onto things | The germs would stay on his hand only
test: A girl in Melbourne has a cold and asks her doctor for antibiotics. What will the doctor probably explain? | Antibiotics do not work on viruses, and rest and fluids help a cold | Antibiotics cure colds in one hour | She needs antibiotics for every sneeze | Antibiotics make the cold spread less to others
test: Why is it a problem to take antibiotics when they are not needed? | They can work less well later when really needed | They taste far too bitter for most children to swallow | They make your hair fall out | They turn into viruses
test: Which is true about the five-second rule? | It is a myth, because germs get on food at once | Food is safe for exactly five seconds | It works only on clean kitchen floors, not on dirty ones | It works for sweets but not for fruit
test: Why does a wet coat make you feel cold so fast? | Being wet takes heat away from the body quickly | Water makes the wind stronger | Wet clothes are heavier, so you get tired | Rain is always colder than snow
test: A family in Auckland starts a beach day in sunshine. Why might they pack jumpers too? | The weather can change during the day | Jumpers keep the sand off their clothes | It is polite to carry jumpers | Jumpers protect from sunburn best
test: Why might you get cold sores from sharing a drink? | The virus spreads through spit on the cup | Cold drinks with ice in them cause cold sores | The cup is too cold for the lips | Sharing makes you sad and sore
test: A girl wants to wear flip-flops on a rocky mountain path in Colorado. Why is that a bad idea? | Her feet need support and protection on the rocks | Flip-flops are too warm for hiking | Flip-flops make you walk too fast | Mountains do not allow sandals
test: Your cousin's shoes from last year still go on but hurt. Why? | Children's feet grow fast and the shoes are now too small | Shoes shrink a size in the cupboard every winter | Old shoes always hurt | Her feet are swollen from walking all day in the cold
test: Why does a tissue or an elbow work better than hands for a sneeze? | Hands touch many things and pass the germs on | Elbows kill germs | Tissues make the sneeze shorter | Hands are already too clean
test: dropped my biscuit, 5 second rule!! | The five second rule is a myth, germs get on at once | Yes, it is safe within five seconds | Yes, if you blow on it first to get the germs off it | Yes, the floor at home is clean
test: nan says dont go out w wet hair u'll catch a cold | Colds come from viruses, not wet hair | Yes, wet hair gives you a cold every time | Yes, but only in winter | Only if your hair is very long
test: got a cold, can i have the antibiotics left from mum | No, antibiotics do not work on viruses | Yes, antibiotics cure colds fast, any kind | Yes, if you take only half | Yes, if they are not out of date
test: achoo! sneezed in my hands lol | Sneeze into a tissue or elbow, and wash your hands | That is fine, hands catch germs | Wipe them on your jumper | Shake your hands to dry them
explain: A boy in Minnesota went out to play in the snow in thin gloves; after an hour his fingers were white and numb with frostnip, and he had to warm them slowly indoors.
explain: A girl with a bad cough kept coughing into the air on the school bus; within a week half her row had caught the same cold.
fact: Frostbite can hurt fingers, toes, ears and noses within minutes in very cold, windy weather, so you wear layers, a hat and gloves.
fact: Fingers that go white and numb from cold are warmed slowly indoors, not rubbed with snow or put under very hot water, and you tell an adult.
fact: A sneeze can spray droplets more than two metres, so you catch it in a tissue or your elbow.
test: its minus 20 out, can i go out in my hoodie | No, dress in layers, hat and gloves, skin can freeze fast | Yes, running about the whole time keeps you warm enough | Yes, hoods are as good as hats | Only cover up if it snows
test: fingers gone white and numb from the snow | Go in, warm them slowly, tell an adult | Rub them hard with snow | Hold them under very hot water | Keep playing until they feel better
test: sneezing loads, just sneeze at everyone innit | No, use a tissue or your elbow so you don't spread it | Yes, sneezes don't carry germs | Yes, if you say sorry after | Sneeze into your hands then shake hands
test: omg a girl coughed all over the bus and the WHOLE school got sick for a month, what's the real lesson | Cough and sneeze into a tissue or your elbow | Never take the bus, it is full of germs | Coughs only spread to people who are weak | Hold your breath when someone coughs nearby
test: nan reckons if u go out with wet hair u'll catch ur death of cold, true? | No, colds come from viruses, not from wet hair | True, wet hair is what causes most colds | True, but only in winter months | True, so wet hair must be dried in the oven
test: kids are saying Ava is "diseased" and nobody should sit near her cos she had a cough, should i spread it | No, it's unkind; just use your own cup and wash your hands | Yes, everyone should stay away from her | Yes, but only to people who sit near her | Yes, and tell the teacher to send her home
exam: Identify the principal reason for which antibiotics are not administered for the common cold. | Colds are caused by viruses, upon which antibiotics have no effect | Antibiotics cure colds too rapidly to be safe | Colds are caused by bacteria too small for antibiotics | Antibiotics are effective only in summer
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the so-called five-second rule concerning dropped food? | It is a myth, as food picks up germs at once | It is accurate, as germs require five seconds to arrive | It is accurate solely on clean kitchen floors | It is accurate provided the food is dry
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the hazard of being wet in cold weather. | Being wet causes the body to lose heat rapidly | Being wet keeps the body warmer than being dry | Being wet is harmless provided one keeps moving | Being wet is a danger solely in summer
exam: Determine the appropriate course of action in respect of a cough that persists for several weeks. | It requires the attention of a doctor | It requires no attention whatsoever | It is to be treated with leftover antibiotics | It is cured by going out with wet hair
